MathCAT (LaTeX braille code): plain LaTeX, written again as LaTeX builds it

Mathessible writes a LaTeX document's equations from their MathML with
MathCAT's LaTeX braille code (an edit in the professional view, a paste,
a document changed to LaTeX), and that LaTeX is built again by
UnicodeMathML's TeX2UnicodeMath. What the code wrote did not always build
back, or was no LaTeX:

- The "ct:"/"tc:" concatenation markers (U+F8FE, U+F8FF) of the unicode
  tables were left in the output (around \mathbf v, before Greek
  letters): LaTeX_cleanup removes them, as speech does.
- Math italic Greek wrote \mathmit, no LaTeX command: lowercase italic
  Greek is LaTeX's own \alpha; capitals are \mathit\Gamma; \mathmit{\nabla}
  and the like are \nabla.
- A function name's limits (lim, max, sup, ...) were \underset{x \to 0}{\lim}:
  they are now \lim_{x \to 0}, as the large operators' are.
- The converter's combining accents (U+20D7, U+0303, ...) were
  \overset{\vec}{v}: they are now accents, \vec{v} and \tilde{z}.
- cases (a "{" before a table, with no closing fence) lost its structure:
  \begin{cases} ... \end{cases}.
- Text (mtext) was written as math letters: \text{...}, its spaces kept
  as spaces (not \space or \~, which are for math).
- A table with no fence before it was bare rows: aligned equations
  (intent :equations) are \begin{aligned} ... \end{aligned}, others
  \begin{matrix} ... \end{matrix}.

Applied by scripts/bootstrap.sh on top of upstream 85c89a37. Each is a bug
in the LaTeX code itself, for braille readers too; worth proposing upstream.

